  "narration": {
    "scriptSha256": "76de467a6fe64316c81abca2f4bca26338003fa6bf82a454297d8f85fa554c46",
    "text": "What if an animal could reverse its own life cycle? This is Turritopsis dohrnii. Normally, attached polyps bud off tiny jellyfish, which grow into swimming adults. But injury or starvation can send an adult another way. Its bell collapses. Its tentacles disappear. It settles into a cyst-like mass. Inside, cells reorganize. Some can change their specialized identity: transdifferentiation. A stolon grows, then polyps. That colony can bud new jellyfish. Life-cycle reversal can happen repeatedly in laboratory cultures. The molecular controls are still being studied. But predators can still kill it. Reversal isn't guaranteed survival. It doesn't make death impossible. It makes the direction of development reversible."
  },
